Plan 12 - the permission-less, co-operative, deterministic, public domain operating system


What is Vans Plan12?
	Vans Plan 12 is a co-operative, high efficiency, ultra low overhead, permission-less and ring-less open-source
	operating system designed 100% in Intel x86 assembler language for the Tiger Lake micro-arch using the IASM Intel
	assembler. Plan 12 is designed for expert and technical users. The system is designed to be a modular framework
	of explicit and deterministic processes represented entirely by files and buffers (This includes hardware and 
	IPC, designed to be as explicit, deterministic, and flexible as possible. 
	
	Vans Plan 12 is designed to simplify all operations into abstracted filesystem I/O. Process management,
	scheduling, process IPC, hardware management, and more, are all abstracted to simply function using a single
	universal format that simplifies everything to I/O.

	Variation 5.0 is the fifth major 
	variation of the operating system. This version changed multiple macro calls, added several macro calls, 
	overhauled the 12P protocol to be more flexible, and added a new control method for SPAWN. Programs written for
	previous versions are... well.. technically they are compatible with 5.0, but it is not recommended that you
	run them, as macros, such as SPAWN will not work properly due to the expected behavior changing. 

	High-level overview

		In Vans Plan 12, there are two types of data that represent everything. They are known as ITEMS and 
		STATES. ITEMS are split into 4 subgroups, being processes, buffers, comms, and system states. An ITEM
		will always be 64B in size, as to fit perfectly inside of one L1 cache line for this CPU arch.
		A STATE will always be 3200B in size. A STATE is a piece of data used to store information on paused
		(YIELDed) processes and how to start them again.

		The system uses an abstraction to store this data known as a Virtual Memory Filesystem (MemFS). This
		is a section of memory formatted as a filesystem specific to holding this process data known as 
		P12VMFSF (Plan 12 Virtual Memory FileSystem Format) abstracted to appear as a physical disk to programs
		running on the system. This allows process management and IPC to appear as standard disk\I/O operations.

		Everything is designed to run in userspace. All processes are the same, and everything is a process.
		Everything is designed to work on the idea of read buffer -> interpret buffer -> write buffer.
		Some examples include IPC - every process communicated by creating a buffer ITEM as a comm to tell
		one process to commit an action. Processes give each-other data by passively broadcasting it by
		creating buffers. Hardware management is simply processes that move data from inside a buffer into/out
		a physical data line.
		Nothing is special, hardware is polled, and there are no interrupts. 

		Vans Plan 12 implements a protocol known as 12VP. This is a universal data sync and transfer protocol
		used to allow one system at the end of a data line to sync disk sectors with another machine without
		knowing the data syncing, the purpose, the data line itself, or anything about the other machines. 
		It can be used to sync disks over network, control hardware of one machine over a serial cable, and
		just about anything you could think of. 

Filesystems
	Vans Plan 12 uses 2 native filesystem formats, being P12VMFSF and P12SDFSF (Plan 12 Virtual Memory FileSystem
	Format and Plan 12 Simple Disk FileSystem Format to each), each holding a very specific purpose within the
	machine. 

	P12VMFSF is used to format memory filesystems to hold process data and control information.
	P12SDFSF is used for general-purpose data storage.

	
	P12VMFSF
		This format is used specifically to store metadata used for process and systems management. It follows
		the format of
		
		[metadata]   - 64B
			- name (ASCII)   - 12B
			- format (ASCII) - 12B
			- size (ASCII)   - 30B
			- future         - 10B	

		[ITEM file]  - (repeater per ITEM on disk)
				[64B  ]  - ITEM metadata  - [info about ITEM and it's data]

		[STATE file] - (repeated per STATE on disk)
				[128B ]  - blank padding  - [marker of STATE - used to separate ITEM from STATE]
				[3200B]  - data inside of STATE
	P12SDFSF
		This is a very simple filesystem - it is literally designed to be as simple as possible.
		
		[metadata]   - 64B
			- name (ASCII)   - 12B
			- format (ASCII) - 12B
			- size (ASCII)   - 30B
			- future         - 10B
		[dir name]   - 32B
		[file name]  - 32B
		[file data]  - variable size
		[end marker] - 64B

		This format is used as a general purpoce data storage device and follows the format of
		This format allows for single-layer directories and basic universal data storage with easily
		parsed metadata and large file sizes

STATES and ITEMS
	Files in a MemFS are placed into two groups, being STATEs and ITEMs.
	
	A STATE is an 3200B piece of metadata that stores information used to CONTINUE a process that was YIELDed.
	An ITEM is a piece of data that is used for process boot keeping or communication. 
					
	ITEMs
		ITEMs are the simpler of the bunch - they are used to define processes, they allow them to communicate,
		they allow them to send information to each-other, and they store information about the system
		that processes frequently use. 

		ITEMs are split into four groups - processes, buffers, comms, and system states.

		Processes
			Processes are simply flat machine code stored in memory with an associated process ITEM to 
			keep track of it. They communicate by creating a comm ITEM requesting another process
			to commit an action, they create and read/write to buffers to give other processes small 
			pieces of information, and they read/modify system state ITEMs to mark the values of a MemFS

			Each MemFS controls one single CPU core and has one process running inside of it at a time. That
			one process holds full execution control of that core. Scheduling is fully co-operative through

			Processes are assigned a permission ID as a numeric . This is used to determine what
			processes are allowed to override another process' memory region or not.
			Processes are assigned a process ID - a 2 digit numerical value stating the order
			they were spawned. This data is used to refer to that process inside of the MemFS
			A process cannot view another process inside of another MemFS without scanning all of memory.
			AKA, the system has native process and core isolation with low overhead.

			Processes follow the cycle of
				See buffer/comm
				Process data
				Write to buffer/comm
				YIELD to another process
				REPEAT

			Format
				[2B ] - ASCII process ID
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[2B ] - ASCII permission ID
				[1B ] - ASCII chars "/"
				[12B] - ASCII process name
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[8B ] - start working memory
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[8B ] - end working memory
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[8B ] - process entry point
				[19B] - blank data padding

				Example - 12/17/net_manager/1000ABCD/1000ACFF/1000B000
				(Or with full dirs) memfs01:dir:12/17/net_manager/1000ABCD/1000ACFF/1000B000
				(In practice, processes are at base dir so there would be no dir: marker)

		Comms/IPC
			A comm file is a file used for process actions. A process will create a comm file to tell another
			process to do something. Essentially, a process will scan for items with a similar structure to 
			this and read their metadata. It is a form of active communication without data storage. 
			
			Format
				[2B ] - ASCII sender ID
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[2B ] - ASCII sender permission ID
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[2B ] - ASCII reciever ID
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[2B ] - ASCII reciever permission ID
				[1B ] - ASCII char "/"
				[16B] - ASCII comm purpose/action
				[36B] - blank data padding 
				
				Example - 12/13/18/11/get_status_net
				(Or with full dirs) 	
		Buffers
			A buffer is a small file used to allow processes to give each-other small pieces of data within 
			64B limits. This is used for passive messaging (unlike IPC, which is active messaging), small
			data transfer, and whatever else the program may want to use them for.

			Format
				[2B ] - ASCII buffer ID [literally the same as a process ID - spawn order]
				[1B ] - ASCII character "."
				[8B ] - ASCII buffer purpose
				[53B] - buffer data
			
			The system will show a buffer as [disk]:[dir]:(ID.[purpose])
			An example would be memfs01:12.net_out
			The data itself has no specific format and it is up to the processes reading and writing to determine
			what format it uses and how to interpret it. 

		System state
			System state is a small file that gives some information used to know what system you are
			looking at and what it is doing. This file contains the machine ID, a 2-digit ASCII set
			designed to identify the machine over network, the controlling process, the process controlling
			that memfs, the execution unit, being the core linked to that memfs, and the default process, being
			the process that CONTINUE will hand off to, used for a process to end without handing off to anything
			specific (this process is usually a shell).

			Format
				[2B] - machine ID - copied from memory address 0x00010001 to 0x00010002 into the memfs by the 
					ADDMEM macro (the macro used to create a virtual memory filesystem of 
					ANY FORMAT - NOT JUST P12VMFSF)
				[2B] - controlling process - the process ID currently in control of the memfs in question.
					Each memfs has their own set of process IDs (buffers are technically
					processes here because they have their own process ID, but practically,
					they are not, since they have no machine code linked to them), so 
					when addressing by cross-memfs you must either specify the exact memory
					sections or the memfs and process ID.
				[2B] - execution unit - the execution unit linked to the memfs - each memfs 
					(or at least every memfs formatted as P12VMFSF) is, by default, 
					linked to an execution unit. The process in control of that memfs
					will always execute only on that execution unit. It is possible for
					a memfs to have the same execution unit as another memfs, but is not 
					currently facilitated. To do such a thing, you would have to modify 
					the metadata to include the memfs name and the process ID
					of the memfs controlling ID. This variation of Plan 12 assumes the 
					user does not need this, as Tiger Lake CPUs always have at least 4 
					execution cores. You can have processes write to null values by 
					leaving this blank, forcing the process to feed into nothing. 
					KEEP IN MIND - THIS IS UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR - I am pretty sure 
					this will feed into a blank pipe, but I am unsure. There it 
					a reasonable chance this will crash the process.
			
				[2B] - default process ID - This is the default process to hand over execution to. 
				It is used for -
					a process that needs to end execution without any remaining jobs. Since
					this operating system must always be in a state of execution, this process
					is the default end process for a program to hand off to when it's life
					cycle is over. The CONTINUE (explained later) YIELDs (also explained later)
					to this program

				[2B] - architecture ID - This is a small ID used to identify the system architecture 
				used for 12P. This is just a small 2-digit ASCII string associated with the 
				microarchitecture of the system. This will allow one system to easily know 
				the architecture of another. Since this system is designed for the Tiger 
				Lake GenuineIntel arch, the only ID is 01. This is just added to allow other 
				Plan 12 systems to interface with Vans 12P if they wish to. The database for
				architecture IDs is stored in memfs02:sysinfo/archid. This file is generally
				copied here by the bootstrap (see Bootstrap for more info).
		
		Process STATEs
			Process states are small snapshots of the registers processes used to be entered again.
