Macros
	The computing distribution known as Vans Plan 12 uses a small system of macros under the
	macro language P12UMSL (PLan 12 Universal Macro Substitution Language). The language is 
	a small text substitution syntax designed around the assembler language. In reality, 
	since it is simple text definition and substitution, it can work for any string of data. 
	However, in practice, it is only really used with common assembler language expansion. 
	Vans Plan 12 uses macros to expand into native assembler language for process scheduling, 
	optimization, and general repetitive tasks. The main macro definition file (glb.macro) is 
	held within the bootstrap binary itself and copied over to the file memfs02:/macro/glb.macro 
	(see the Bootstrap section for more information). The system comes with a utility known as 
	EXPAND (placed in memfs02:/VP12EF/EXPAND by bootstrap) used to expand macros from source 
	files into expanded code. The utility takes two arguments. Argument one being the 
	file where all macros used are defined, and the second file being the source program 
	where macros are called. For more information on the EXPAND utility, see the Utilities 
	section of documentation.

	The macro syntax follows a few simple primitives - everything inside of a definition 
	statement can be added in place of the associated call statement.
		
		#DEFINE,(name)       - defines macro
		@CALL,(name),[ARGS]  - calls macro
		%ARG[argname]	     - sets argument to location
		&ENDSEC,(name)       - ends definition statement
		
	Everything inbetween a define statement and an endsec statement will replace 
	everything with stating call of the same name.

		Example -
			defining file
				#DEFINE,item
				    MOV RAX, %ARG1
				&ENDSEC,item
			calling file
				@CALL,item,10
			command
				EXPAND (defining_file) (calling file)

		This will replace the line '@CALL,item,10' in the calling file with the line 
		'MOV RAX, 10'. The language is designed to be as simple as possible.
	
	memfs02:/macro/glb.macro
		This is the macro file (this file is optional - it is only required if 
		the system will be building assembler language programs from source code) 
		that defines basic system functions like process scheduling. The file is 
		a small ASCII text file copied to memfs02:/macro/glb.macro by the bootstrap
		code from the boot binary (see the Bootstrap section
		for more detail). This file defines the macros
		
			SPAWN (name) (memfs) (permission) (executable) (start memory) (end memory) (entry)
				SPAWN is the macro used to spawn a raw machine code binary into a process. 
				If said binary is not formatted as VP12EF (also known as Vans 
				Plan 12 Executable Format), then the program would use this macro 
				to execute said flat machine code binary.
					Name       - name of process to be spawned
					MemFS      - MemFS to spawn said process in
					Executable - location of the binary for said program
					Permission - permission to give the new process
					start      - Start memory location to give said process
					end 	   - End memory location to give said process
					entry	   - Memory entry for said process
				
				Example = @CALL,SPAWN,user_shell,memfs01,3,memfs02:/VP12EF/shell,0x0008000,0x0008025,0x0008002
				This example will
					Check if these memory sections are already allocated by scanning 
					all memfs process metadata
						- if it is already allocated it performs a permission check		

							- if the process currently being spawned has a 
							lower permission than the ones in control of that 
							memory region, SPAWN will call KILL on the processes 
							and continue the SPAWN protocol.
							- else, SPAWN will simply exit and not spawn said program. 
					Zero out said memory ranges
					Find the process ID (greatest ID in said memFS +1))
					create a memFS entry with the associated metadata
					copy the metadata of the process to the first location 
					of the start memory address the process specifies.					

				Now, said process is spawned and ready to be executed.
				
			SPAWND (name) (memfs) (permission) (executable)
				SPAWND is the same as SPAWN, besides the fact that SPAWND 
				assumes the metadata for the missing args exists. The most 
				common case for this would be if the binary follows VP12EF, 
				and the data asked in the missing args is stored in the binary 
				metadata. See 'Supported executable formats' for more 
				information of VP12EF. 
				
			START (id) (memfs)
				START is the macro used to begin execution of a process 
				that has not previously started. It will simply read the 
				metadata of said process, update the system state to show 
				what process is currently in control, and begin execution 
				of that process from the entry point stated in said process's 
				metadata. NOTE - SYSTEM STATE IS ALWAYS UPDATED LAST

				The reason this macro exists is so SPAWN can set up 
				multiple processes in series before starting their 
				execution. This allows for round-robin like chains 
				as well as processes that need to be allocated but 
				not yet spawned. This macro used to be embedded into 
				SPAWN, executing the code as soon as SPAWN is called. 
				The addition of this macro adds a lot of flexibility 
				into the system.

				The cycle would essentially go
					SPAWN process
					YIELD running process
					START SPAWNed process

			YIELD (id) (memfs)
				YIELD is used to stop a running process. It will essentially 
				save the process information by creating a process state for 
				it and prepare for execution of another process. It is used 
				to hand execution to another process.
					- Running process calls YIELD
					- This will create a process state for said process
					- and hand off control to the process specified by ID.
					
				Ordering
					- Scans process metadata
					- Creates a process state for the process calling YIELD
					- hands control off to another process (specified by ID
					and MEMFS args)

				WARNING - DO  - NOT-  HAVE A PROCESS YIELD A PROCESS INSIDE OF 
				A DIFFERENT MEMFS - THIS WILL CAUSE UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR
							(I mean you can if you want - nothing is stopping you)
				Refer to the documentation for PROCESS STATEs for more information on this macro.

			SAVE (id) (memfs)
				This macro is used to create a process state without executing 
				another process - essentially it will save process data without 
				stopping the process. This is essentially creating a "snapshot" 
				of said process, just without the memory the process uses. 
				This is very useful for debugging. The reason why YIELD does 
				not just execute one of these states is simply 
				because there would be no one to call a starting macro. By say, 
				the YIELD macro is used to end execution of one process and 
				start another, so if you split that into two parts, there 
				would be no process to call the second macro. This is why YIELD exists.
					This macro will
						- create a process state for the specified process
							- nothing else - it just continues execution.

			CONTINUE (memfs)
				This process is essentially just YIELD, but instead of handing off 
				to the specified process, it will hand off to the default process 
				mentioned in the system state information.

			KILL (id) (memfs)
				This macro is used to KILL a process - running or not.
				This process will check the process metadata
				to see the memory locations and zero them out. It will 
				then remove the process metadata for said process.
			        This macro does not execute anything after it ends, so 
				please do not kill running processes. Please YIELD
				or stop a process, then KILL it. If you KILL a process that 
				holds control, then you will need to SPAWN a 
				process from a user shell on another memfs, as that memfs 
				will be left without a controlling process.
			
			FALL (memfs)
				This macro will calculate the lowest process ID in said 
				memfs and YIELD to it. It is that simple.

			
		Other Macros
			MOVE (source) (dest)
				This is a general-purpose high-efficiency data movement macro. This 
				macro is used to split large chunks of data in a highly efficient
				way, while not leaving the L1 cache. 
				This macro will
					- make source
					- create a data pointer and set it to the last 64B of source
					- take the data mentioned at the data pointer and incriment 
					the data pointer by 64 
						- repeat last line 4 times total to get 4 64B chunks
					- send all 64B chunks to the dest
					- wait 2 cycles to allow the system some breathing room (lol)
					- clear data registers
					- repeat until there is no more data in source
				This data movement macro is designed to move lots of data very 
				quickly without leaving the L1 cache. 
				This macro is very important and is used for ALL DATA MOVEMENT of THE SYSTEM
	
			ADDMEM (start) (end) (name) (format) (core) (default id)
				This is the macro used to create a virtual memory filesystem of any filesystem format.
				It will zero out the memory locations (DOES NOT CHECK IF THEY ARE ALREADY IN USE)
				Format the memory regions 	
				Create the system state for that memfs (ONLY IF IT IS P12VMFSF)
					- copy the machine ID from memory (see Bootstrap for more information)
					- copy the architecture ID from memory (see Bootstrap for more information)
					- set the default ID and core
					- leave out the current running process (since there is not one yet)
					- set the default ID
					- initalize the core and run looping code on 
						this core until the default process is made
					- move the current memfs name (ascii string) to RAX 


				exit (this macro is fairly simple)
				
				Keep in mind this does mean RAX is not to be used by programs.

			PARSESTATE (name) (memfs)
				This will parse the STATE file in a P12VMFSF and output the location of the file as
				R8 and path as R9. If the file being searched for does not exist, it is created at 
				the next free block on the filesystem. 
				It wil- well this should be pretty self-explanitory.

			PARSEITEM (name) (memfs)
				Exactly the same as PARSESTATE, but with an ITEM.

			INCASCII (location)
				This will incriment an ASCII value by 1.
				Location - location in memory of ASCII value
			
			DECASCII (location)
				Same as INCASCII but decrimenting

Software
	Vans Plan 12 has a small set of versatile software 
	written in VP12EF. Every single piece of software, code, 
	and documentation associated with Vans Plan 12 and Plan 
	12 is 100% free and public domain. This is also true for 
	items not within that range but still packaged with the 
	system (such as logos and technical tips). This is a major
	point of this operating system - to be 100% free, open, 
	and public domain.
		
	The system has a few basic utilities included. These are
		EDIT - the basic visual editor program, supporting ASCII and hexodecimal formats.
			Syntax - EDIT {ASCII|HEX} (file)
				ASCII - edits file with ASCII encoding
				HEX   - edits file with hexidecimal encoding
			This program is simply a small vi-like visual editor program. 
			It is very barebones - it  just creates a file named (file).edt
			in the directory of the file behind edited. Most vi commands are 
			entact, but some are not. 
				: - command interface
				w - write file
				q - exit

				MODES
					command - CTRL + c
					Edit    - CTRL + c + i

			That is it. The editor is very simple, but it gets the job done	
		MOVE - the general file movement utility
			Syntax - MOVE {REMOVE|(source)} (dest)
				REMOVE - remove file at (dest)			

			MOVE is just a utility wrapper around the MOVE 
			macro. It will copy data from one location to 
			another. If arg1 is set as REOMVE, it will delete 
			the data at arg2. If not, it copies arg1 to arg2. 
			This is a file movement and copy utility. 
		
		ASSEMBLE - basic flat machine code assembler using IASM syntax (produces flat machine code)
			Syntax - ASSEMBLE (input)	
			
			This is a basic flat machine code assembler written for
			Vans Plan 12 based on the IASM assembler. It is a basic text
			substutution assembler that only produces flat machine 
			code binaries. It will always place the output to the dir the
			input is in at the file name ASMBL.out
	
		FORMAT - disk format utility (formats a disk)
			Syntax - FORMAT {P12SDFSF|P12VMFSF} {MEM|BUF} {(startmem,endmem)|(buffer)}
				arg1 - format
				arg2 - disk or memory
					BUF - disk buffer
					MEM - memory start/end
				arg3 - what to format
					if BUF - buffer file
					if MEM - start,end memory
			
			This utility will write all blanks to the disk buffer to 
			clear it, then format it to the desired filesystem. 	
			If the MEM option is specified, the system formats the 
			start and end memory locations. If the BUF is specified,
			it will format the disk buffer.

		MAKE - binary conversion tool
			Syntax - MAKE (source) (startmem) (endmem) (entry)	
			This utility will convert a flat machine code binary into 
			VP12EF. The source must be a flat machine code binary.
			This utility will always write to MKE.out as a complete VP12EF binary. 
			It uses the data in the arguments to set the metadata for the file.
		
		EXPAND - macro expansion tool
			Syntax - EXPAND (source) (macro file)
			
			This utility will expand all macros in the source arg and 
			replace them with the associated macro in the macro file arg.
			This utility is just a text substitution tool. This tool 
			expects assembler source code as arg1 and a macro definition text
			file (ASCII) as arg2. This tool will always place output at EXPND.out.

		SHELL - basic command line interpretation program
			This really should not need explanation - all it does is execute 
			command line arguments.
			The command RUN will run a program
				RUN (path to program)
				Example - > RUN memfs02:VP12EF:MOVE disk1:file disk2:dir:file.pout	
			There is no universal 'quit' like on UNIX. Why would you need 
			one? Just allow the running program to terminate itself.
			That is it. The shell prints the characters '> ' as a prompt 
			and waits for an associated value. 
			As of 5.0, RUN is the only valid value, and I currently do not 
			see a reason to add more. The only reason this is a value
			in the first place is to allow the system to remain explicit. 
			Print '> ' when ready and get values.

the 12P protocol
	The 12P protocol is an example of how a simple system can diverge to 
	allow a complex issue to be solved using simple primitives. The 12P protocol
	allows for method-independent universal sector-level data transfer and disk sync. 
	As long as the machine has a machine ID and it is connected via any data 
	line that allows both systems to communicate, this protocol can work. 

	The protocol is designed to allow one system to share and sync a 
	portion of their disk with another across a data line without the 
	system knowing what the data is, the data line in question, or 
	anything about the machine in question besides the machine ID. 
	The system simply messages the end of the data line and asks the 
	machine some questions to know how to send said data.

	Now this is the point in the documentation where the system begins to get very 
	technical. This, as well as the bootstrap sections, will be
	the most important sections, as they show real implementation.


	12P is a protocol used to sync sectors of memory across multiple remote machines 
	without having any information on the other machines in the chain. 
	This protocol works over any two-way data line.

		Machine A sends request packets down data line, requesting all machines to 
		reply with their machine ID, OS version and 12P version. Machine A receives 
		this information and asks a specific machine (by their machine ID) for 
		metadata of their disk (start/end sectors and their metadata for every 
		partition). Machine A then asks for a specific start and end disk sector 
		from said machine to be synced. Machine A copies this data to a location 
		(usually memory - can be any location) and mounts it as a memFS of that 
		same format

		When ever a system makes a write to that disk, a special manager managing 
		this will send a packet across the machine sending the updated sectors
		and the new data to place into these sectors. 

	You may ask, what if multiple machines write to the same sectors at the same time? The 
	updating is order-oriented, so you will receive the update and be prompted by a shell 
	asking if you want to write or not write said data. Simply don't allow your copy to become 
	invalid. If you are writing data as they are sending, simply give a new output location 
	(write prompt specifies out location) and analyze the data and make your changes. 

	This protocol is designed to work with only memory locations. Older versions of 12P allow for both memory and
	disk, but as of the change made in 5.6 removing disk filesystem abstractions, disks are no longer consitored
	'first-class' entities. I would rather not support something instead of having poor support for it.


	12P follows a numeric version scheme using 8 bytes total. The version documented here, 
	being 01.00/00 The first two numbers display the major version - second two display the 
	minor version - third the architecture ID.
		For more information on the Arch ID, see Bootstrap, Filesystems, and ITEMs. 	
		
	AKA, for a 5.6 Vans Plan 12 system, the data will always be 01.00/01
			01.00 is the current 12P version and 01 is the arch ID for GenuineIntel Tiger Lake
	
		Packet formats
			The first packet will always be a packet sent from one machine to the 
			data line itself. This packet is used to tell other machines
			to identify themselves and request metadata on the machine itself.
			This packet is formatted as such
				[2B ] - marker
				[2B ] - machine ID
				[8B ] - 12P version 
				[38B] - blank padding

					The marker is a piece of data used to signify that the 
					packet is used for 12P
					The string will ALWAYS be 0000 0000 1101 0011 - this 
					string is used to identify 12P packets.
						AKA, for a standard 5.0.0 system on 12P 01.00 
						on Vans Plan 12 with a machine ID of A8
						The packet would be
							0000 0000 1101 0001
							0100 0001 0011 1000
							0011 0000 0011 0001 0010 1110 0011 
								0000 0011 0000 0010 1111 0011 0000 0011 0001
							[38 blank bytes]

						The first 16B conveys this is a 12P packet -
						the next 2B convey the machine ID in ASCII - the next 8B convey
						the arch and version of 12P in ASCII and the
						rest is blank.

			Next, every machine at the other end of said line will send a return packet 
			with the same format down the other end of the data line to that
			machine sending.
				The only difference is that the marker, unlike the sender being a 
				first stage request packet, this is a first stage sender packet.
				The marker would be 0001 1001 0011 1001 - this shows it is a 
				reply to the first packet sent. 

				The machine sends their machine ID, their 12P version and this
				string in the same format as the last packet.

			Next, the same machine that sent the first packet will send a packet to 
			the machine it wants to request data from. The format for this packet
			goes
				[2B ] - marker
				[2B ] - machine ID sender
				[2B ] - machine ID receiver
				[58B] - padding
					The marker for this packet type is 0000 0010 1101 1100
					This packet is used to request metadata information from one 
					machine to be sent to another. This data includes the
						- metadata for each sector or filesystem on the 
						location specified by bytes 6 to 7.
						- the start and end sectors for each partition 
						(or memory if the item is 02)
					This packet in reference is used to allow the first system 
					to know what is where and what they want to sync. 
						The format of said packet goes
							[2B  ] - marker [0010 1101 1101 0010]
							(for every partition)
								[metadata]
								[start sector] (or memory)
								[end sector]   (or memory)
								[64B blank]
							[128B] - blank marker

						This packet is used to give the first system 
						the information they need to know what would be where.
						One of these packets is sent for every disk on said machine.

					The machine will then send a packet that is used to 
					request specific sectors of the disk to be synced. 

					This packet follows the format
						[2B ] - marker [0010 1101 1111 1101]
						[2B ] - machine ID sending to
						[2B ] - machine ID sender
						[start location]
						[end location]
						[data itself]
						[64B end data marker - blank]
					The machine will then copy this data to memory and 
					mount it as a memfs. A file named memfs02:12P.tbl keeps track of 
					the associated memfs to their sector IDs and the 
					machine ID of the machine that send the data and the machine ID
					of the current machine.
					The other machine also does the same.

						Any time a machine writes to this data, a 
						process will broadcast the last item in the same format, just
						with the marker 0010 1101 1101 1111. This 
						allows for all machines to keep a copy of the data.
						All syncs are managed in the same way as any 
						other process trying to send data to a data line.

			The tables for marker mappings is stored at memfs02:tables:12Pmap


	And there it is. The 12P protocol. 

		This protocol is an example of how a simple system allows for complex issues to be 
		solved in a simple way. 12P allows for universal
		data transfer to any Plan 12 system that supports Vans 12P across a bidirectional 
		data line.


Hardware management
	Hardware is managed by a small set of normal processes. By bootstrap, these are mapped to memfs03.
	Hardware is managed by what is known as a hardware manager process. This process generally 
	has an in and out buffer. Whatever data is inside of said out buffer(s)
	is simply sent to the data line as-is.

		In memfs03 you would have a generic hardware manager - this process is used to 
		convert generic hardware names to specific hardware names based on the hardware 
		lookup table copied to memfs02:tables:hrdwrtbl - this file is a translation file that 
		converts generic hardware names to specific names
		(eg. DISPLAY to VESA). 
			A program that would need to, say, display text, would write to the 
			DISPLAY.out (memfs03:(00).display.out)						
			The hardware manager will then parse the lookup table - try to 
			find the line display.out - it will then copy whatever data is in that
			buffer to the associated buffer in the mapping file.
			The hardware manager for that item will then broadcast that data 
			to the correct data port (the port itself is baked into the binary).

			This allows for programs to not know the specific hardware.
				There is a generic buffer in every memfs that the program of 
				that memfs writes to and a generic manager specific to each memfs.
				The memfs generic writes to memfs03:(), where the manager for 
				that memfs writes to the specific buffer. This also allows for programs
				to not need to leave their memfs.

			program - writes to generic buffer of their memfs
			mapper  - copies said data to memfs03:(buffer)
			mapper2 - copies the data in memfs03:(buffer) to the associated 
			hardware manager buffer by parsing the mapper table (memfs02:tbl.map)
			the hardware manager for that specific data line broadcasts the data.
	
		It is that simple.

	Also, hardware uses its own scheduler. As stated, the primitives provided allow the 
	system to create any scheduler. Memfs03 uses a round-robin scheduler. 
	It does this by having every process work on a system like this
		- when given control
			- check if work needs to be done (check buffers and comms)
				- if none, hand to current ID + 1
				- if work, do work
				- hand to current ID + 1
		
		At the end of the chain there is a dummy process that calls FALL to restart the chain 
		whenever it gets control.

		As shown fully by this, the scheduler primitives allow the system to use any 
		kind of scheduling. Actually, this is the entire point of the memfs system - they 
		are not designed for process isolation, but scheduler isolation. Each memfs is 
		linked to one execution unit (a core or a thread of a machine ID), allowing each 
		execution unit to use it's own scheduler depending on what the memfs may require.
		This is a major design point of this system. A scheduler is just a primitive that allows
		processes to manage how they want to be scheduled. 

	Please note that disks do not allow for standard filesystem abstraction, instead expose sector I/O
	via the hardware manager's buffers.
	Disks will have a hardware manage program with an input buffer and output buffer. This buffer is used to 
	give the manager commands. 

	When a process needs to commit an action, it will write the action as a comm file to request the disk manager 
	to make an action. This is basically sending a command to the item.

	The protocol to read a disk will go

		Process needs to read disk
			Process writes sectors it needs to the disk manager buffer
				Format of (ASCII) '(action)/(ar1).(arg2).(arg3)'
					Action tells what to do with the data
							Actions include 'read' to copy sectors of disk to memory
									'write' to copy the sections of memory to sectors

									For a read, arg1 represents the start sector, arg2 
										    represents the end sector, and arg3
										    represents the memory location to copy to

									For a write, arg1 represents the start memory where the 
										     data is, arg2 represents the end memory where
										     the data is, and arg3 represents the start 
										     sector to copy to 

			Process creates a comm file to the disk manager to tell it to read it's input buffer
				Format with a name (desc/purpose) of 'read_input'
			Process waits until it finds a comm stating it is done

			Disk manager sees comm file 
			Disk manager reads it's input buffer 
			Disk manager reads said sectors and copies it to the memory location specified
			Disk manager creates a comm file with the text '(action)_done' (here it would be 'read_done')

			Process sees comm file stating it is complete - the raw sectors of the disk have been displayed.


		If the process also needs to read a filesystem on the disk, they will send a request to the filesystm parser program. This
		is a program that can understand a single fileystem. The program is pointed to raw disk sectors in memory and will output
		a structure of directories and files based on that data. It will parse the filesystem metadata to output information such as
		name, permission (if included), date (if included), and most importantly, it will state the location on disk (the start/end sectors)
		of each file and dir and it's metadata. This is used to know what files are where and what data they may hold. Keep in mind the
		parser does not show the data in each file, just the location where the data is plus the parsed metadata. 
		Every filesystem parser works a little differently due to differences in filesystems. The only one that is known to be 'standard' 
		is the filesystem parser for P12SDFSF. This parser expects such items as it's format

		An input buffer with needed data
		a comm stating to commit an action

		The input buffer should be formatted as such
			(filesystem)/(start mem).(end mem).(output mem)

			The filesystem type tells the disk parser the type of filesystem used. This is generally only P12SDFSF, but was included for
			future flexibility. 
			The start mem is the start memory location of the filesystem data
			The end mem is the end memory location of the filesystem data
			The output mem is the start memory location to write the parsed data to

		The comm file is expected to simply state 'read_input'
		The comm file to show completion is simply 'parse_complete'

		The format for the output data goes

			(for every item on disk)
				dirname it is in
				file name
				file sector location (start)
				file sector location (end)
				(padding to nearest (rounded up) multiple of 64 if not already)

		The sizes of each item depends on the filesystem type itself

		This program is used for processes to parse filesystem information.



	Anyways, I am sure you can guess what reading data would be.


Bootstrap
	This section will explain the bootstrap process of the system. The bootstrap is a small section 
	of the code from the main distribution binary. The point of this code is to set up the basic 
	execution environment used by all programs that will run after it. The bootstrap is not, in itself,
	a process. It is the only item in this system that is not represented. This is because it is used 
	to set up the environment used to allow all of the cool stuff this operating system can do.

	The purpose of the bootstrap code is to set up basic hardware options, set up hardware manager processes, 
	set up execution, and hand control to the user. The bootstrap will disable non-required services, like
	interrupts, before executing. 

	The bootstrap is split into six stages. These are simply named 'Stage 0' to 'Stage 5' (A bootstrap
	does not need special naming for stages).

	

	Stage 0 - initialization
		This is the phase where the system is no longer bare metal UEFI and is now Plan 12. This is
		the hand-off phase, where control is shifted from UEFI to the operating system. Some basic
		unused features are disabled here. These include hardware interrupts (disabled later) and 
		just about every EFI service

	Stage 1 - setup
		This is the phase where basic hardware is enabled. This stage depends entirely on what is 
		pre-programmed, but it usually consists of PS/2 keyboard, serial, and sometimes display.
		Sometimes this step is skipped over completely, as it is not needed, and hardware would later
		be managed by just the hardware managers to come about later. This is very common in headless
		or expert user setups. This, however, does make debugging nearly impossible. If you wish to
		go this route, please, be careful and confident.

	Stage 2 - mapping
		This is the stage where memory is set up. This stage will zero out all pre-programmed memory 
		ranges (just in-case) and use the ADDMEM macro to allocate the correct memfs containers.

		        First, the machine ID is created - this is a random 2 character English ASCII ID
                        that is unique to this machine at this boot. 
			- 	(UNLESS SPECIFIED IN EXTRA DATA - SEE 'Extra data' FOR MORE INFO)	 -
			It is used for the 12P protocol to 
                        execute instructions on different machines. Without it, if you mounted one disk
                        as a memfs via 12P and started a process, said process would start on both machines.
                        This ID is stored at physical location 0x00010001 to 0x00010002 and copied to 
			memfs02:tables:machine.id
			The arch ID (more info in 12P and Extra data) is copied to memfs02:tables:arch.id in stage 5.

                                - 0x20000000 to 0x20200000 (2MB ) - MemFS01 region
                                - 0x20200000 to 0x20400000 (2MB ) - MemFS02 region
                                - 0x20400000 to 0x20800000 (4MB ) - MemFS03 region
				- 0x20800000 to 0x23600000 (48MB) - MemFS04 region

                        The ADDMEM macro is used here to allocate the memory filesystems - syntax is 

                                @CALL,ADDMEM,0x20000000,0x20200000,memfs01,p12vmfsf,1,1
                                @CALL,ADDMEM,0x20200000,0x20400000,memfs02,p12vmfsf,1,1 
                                @CALL,ADDMEM,0x20400000,0x20600000,memfs03,p12vmfsf,2,2
				@CALL,ADDMEM,0x20800000,0x23600000,memfs04,p12sdfsf

		Keep in mind these memory ranges are not strict - this is just the default amount
		for a standard desktop system. The regions can be much lower than this (few KB), but this is
		the default allocation. This allocation is very generous in range allocation.

		The 'working space' section is a P12SDFSF MemFS used as a 'disk'. It is used for output items
		for most programs. The default ammount for this is 48MB, as that is how much I (the average user)
		would be using. If this is not enough for you, modify the bootstrap to create a larger one or
		create one after boot.

		The reason the system never allocates core 0 is because this core must run the code to disable
		interupts at the end of bootstrap. If this core was allocated, nothing would be able to do this.
		No, I would like for this not to be a process.
		So far, I am unsure about how to resolve this issue, so the architecture currently cannot use 
		core 0 unless sceduled manually by a bare metal application. The bootstrap only allocates cores
		1 and 2 for now, leaving 0 and 3 (0 at HALT and 3 never started) unallocated and unused. Core 
		3 can be allocated during bootstrap, but I have not done this becuase I have no personal reason
		to at this moment. 
		Anyways, I would rather core 0 be unused than it be initalized in a different manner than cores 1-3.

	Stage 3 - setup
		This is the phase where programs are placed in the correct place. memfs02 is a location used to store
		programs. The bootstrap binary stores a small disk image of the Plan 12 simple disk file system format
		on the binary - this image contains binaries for the base set of programs. 
		The process goes
			- Search the boot binary (wherever UEFI decides to copy it to lol)
			- Find the first 64B blank marker
			- Copy everything from that 64B blank marker to the 128B blank marker (the disk image)
		It is really that simple. Just a small parse and call of the MOVE macro.
		This binary has a layout like this
			memfs02:
				- hardware:
					- all hardware manager programs
				- VP12EF:
					- user programs formatted as VP12EF (most)
				- flat:
					- user programs written in flat machine code

			example - memfs02:VP12EF:shell


	Stage 4 - hardware spawning
		This is the stage where hardware managers and chains are organized and started. 
		Every item in memfs02:hardware is named by (order).(name) and formatted as VP12EF.
		The bootstrap will SPAWND to each item in this folder (this includes the round-robin FALL loop process)
		and then START on the first ID. 
		This allows for hardware to be fully managed and set up. This is the phase where the system is 
		cons Plan 12 and no longer bootstrap.

	Stage 5 - Exit
		This is the stage where the bootstrap exits. The bootstrap will clear the data in it's tmp space 
		The arch ID (more info in 12P and Extra data) is copied to memfs02:tables:arch.id. 
		Finally, the system will execute
			
			@CALL,SPAWND,shell,1,memfs02:VP12EF:shell

		This - right here - marks the end of the bootstrap.
		The bootstrap has officially exited and the system is now fully 100% Vans Plan 12.

		
		The reason interupts are disabled at exit is because the ADDMEM macro enables the core. This means
		all cores holding processes must be started at bootstrap. A memfs can be created after, but cannot
		run code.		

	That is the bootstrap process. It is very simple and designed to be able to be implemented in less than
	300 lines of code.

	The format for the bootstrap binary goes
		[first section ] - Bootstrap code itself    - variable size
		[second section] - blank marker             - 64B
		[third section ] - memfs02 image (P12SDFSF) - variable size
		[fourth section] - extra data               - 64B

	Extra data 
		The extra data refers to some small pre-set information.

		The format for this goes
		[2B] - machine ID - if 00, generate at boot. Else, the data here will be the machine ID.
		[2B] - arch ID    - this 2 char system is explained in 12P and is used to identify system arch.




Supported executable binary formats
	This system supports two formats - 
		Native VP12EF (Vans Plan 12 Executable Format) and flat, static machine code.
		VP12EF is a format designed to make SPAWND easy. It is very self-explanatory.


	Item 1 - metadata
        [0-7B]   - start memory location
        [8-15B]  - end memory location
        [16-23B] - memory entry point
        - this item is scanned by SPAWN to get the information mentioned previously.

        Item 2 - debug (OPTIONAL)
        - this is repeated per macro
        [0-15B]  - macro name
        [16-21B] - instruction macro takes place at (number)
        [22-63B] - padding
        - this data is used for debugging to know what and where the macros of the binary are

        Item 3 - blank
        [0-63B]  - blank 
        - this item marks the end of the metadata

        Item 4 - code
        - this section is just flat machine code

        Overhead - 128B minimum
        Plus 64B optional per macro for debugging

	This is how VP12EF works. Very simple


Usage
	This section is optional depending on the user. The average user should most read this. This section
	will explain how to properly use a Vans Plan 12 system. This can be ignored if the system is implemented 
	in an embedded environment without a human present. 

	Shell 
		This is the common user interface utility. This utility provides basic macro substitution from the
		memfs02:tables:shell.cmd. Everything written in this file is included into the shell as an extra option.
		The defaults for this include RUN as an expansion for SPAWND and PRINT as an expansion for a longer macro.
		PRINT was not specified in the utilities section, as it is not normally used. PRINT is a long macro chain 
		used to display text onto a screen at user command. The PRINT item simply moves the PRINT data into
		the buffer (memfs):(id).printout. This buffer is used to store data commanded by the PRINT command that is
		going to be printed to the display.

		The shell uses the $ARG tag to supply shell arguments provided as simple text substitution.
		The shell also uses a file in memfs02:tables:shell.args to define global arguments. 
		The syntax for this file goes
			[name]:[value]
		
			Example -
			CURRENT_MEMFS:memfs02
			CURRENT_ID:1

		CURRENT_MEMFS - the memfs the shell is spawned in.
		CURRENT_ID - the ID of the current shell

		Keep in mind - none of these are set by default. These are only set by the spawner of the shell.
		These are set as a global item by the bootstrap, as the system is expected to only have one shel.

		Arguments are ended by the char "|"
		After this char, the shell will know "the next statement is not part of an argument"
		This is optional and is only to be used if part of a single line is to be used as an argument.

		The syntax for said definition file simply goes
			[name]:[expansion]

			Example -
			RUN:@CALL,SPAWND,$1,$2,$3,$4
			PRINT:@CALL,MOVE,$1,$CURRENT_MEMFS|$CURRENT_ID|.printout

		These items are used to expand the shell commands RUN and PRINT.
		PRINT will have the shell call the MOVE macro to move data from input to the display.
		RUN is used to expand into assembler and spawn a process.


		PRINT is a bit more complex. The associated buffer for the printing was added to the shell 
		for this purpose only.
		This buffer is really just a midway point for data about to be moved to the display by the shell.
		It is only used for data from the PRINT command. For anything else, the generic shell.out buffer
		is used.

		The workflow goes

		shell.input -> MOVE (shell) -> shell.print -> MOVE (shell print manager) -> display generic -> 
		MOVE (hardware allocator) -> specific display -> MOVE (hardware manager for display) -> data line 
		-> screen buffer (memory - depends on screen type)

		The chain allows for any step to be replaced or modified and for every step to be traceable and
		easily debugged. Example - if the input is not from PRINT, just modify the shell buffers used.
		This chain should be used as a guide onto how process should communicate to each-other.

		The shell uses these specific buffers
			- shell.print - data being printed to display via PRINT
			- shell.out   - data being used as output for shell
			- shell.in    - data being used as input for shell

	Edit
		The edit utility is a vi-like interactive hex and ASCII text editor utility.
		This utility will create the file memfs04:/edit.tmp - this is the file currently being edited. 
		When the user is ready to submit changes, the data from this point is moved from this buffer to the 
		file specified by :wl (location). 

		Essentially, the editor works like this
			- text held in the input buffer of edit (edit.input)
				- at the newline printing, it is moved to the display buffer without removing data, causing 
				  the text to be printed one line at a time.
			- at the command :w, the data is copied from the display buffer to memfs04:/edit.tmp
			- at the command :wl (location), the data from said temporary buffer is moved to the location specified.

		This editor is designed to have vi-like syntax - CTRL+C to enter into.. uh. mode selector.. yea let's call it that.
		Then : to move from mode selector to command mode

		From command mode, you have
			- i  - enter edit mode
			- w  - write to buffer
			- wl - write to location
			- q  - exit program 

		The workflow would generally go

			
			(Example - 'Hello' is currently in the input buffer)
			(types 'World!')
				ASCII 'World!' is moved to the input buffer
				when the program detects a newline printer, it will write the new data to the
				display buffer

				This causes the display to go from

					1. Hello

				to
					1. Hello
					2. World!

			The editor is designed to be very simple, intuitive, and vi-like.
